.\"
.\" POSIX_OPENPT(3) - Substrate libc
.\"
.TH POSIX_OPENPT 3 "2026-05-06" "Substrate OS" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
posix_openpt, grantpt, unlockpt, ptsname, ptsname_r \- Unix98 pseudo-terminal API
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RB ( libc ", " -lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.BI "int posix_openpt(int " flags );
.BI "int grantpt(int " fd );
.BI "int unlockpt(int " fd );
.BI "char *ptsname(int " fd );
.BI "int ptsname_r(int " fd ", char *" buf ", size_t " buflen );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR posix_openpt ()
opens
.B /dev/ptmx
and returns a file descriptor referring to a freshly-allocated
pseudo-terminal master.  The corresponding slave is reachable at
.B /dev/pts/<N>
once
.BR unlockpt ()
has been called.  The slave's
.I N
is reported by
.BR ptsname ().
.PP
.BR grantpt ()
is a no-op on Substrate; the kernel grants ownership of the slave to
the caller's UID at allocation time.  Returns 0 on success, or \-1 on
error (with
.I errno
set).
.PP
.BR unlockpt ()
clears the slave's allocation lock by issuing
.BR TIOCSPTLCK (2)
with a value of 0.  Until the lock is cleared, opening the slave
returns
.BR EIO ,
which guards against a concurrent process opening a slave that's
about to be reused.
.PP
.BR ptsname ()
returns a pointer to a static buffer naming the slave (e.g.
.BR /dev/pts/3 ).
.BR ptsname_r ()
writes the same name into the caller-supplied buffer and is the
thread-safe form.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR posix_openpt ()
returns the master fd, or \-1 on failure.
.BR grantpt (),
.BR unlockpt ()
return 0 on success, \-1 on failure.
.BR ptsname ()
returns a pointer or
.BR NULL .
.BR ptsname_r ()
returns 0 on success, an
.B errno
value on failure.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBADF
.I fd
is not an open PTY master.
.TP
.B ENOTTY
.I fd
does not refer to a Unix98 PTY master.
.TP
.B ERANGE
.RB ( ptsname_r ())
.I buflen
is too small to hold the slave's name.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
int m = posix_openpt(O_RDWR);
grantpt(m);
unlockpt(m);
char *slave = ptsname(m);
int s = open(slave, O_RDWR);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (2),
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR pts (4)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2008 (XSI option).
